What this covers
----------------

`specs/`, `articles/`, and `methodology/`, which is the same scope the previous
combined LICENSE stated. All three directories exist in this repository.

What it does not cover
----------------------

The software: `python/`, `javascript/`, `ruby/`, `principles/`, and the
reference implementations generally. Those are Apache 2.0. See LICENSE.

Code examples embedded inside documentation are part of the software and carry
the Apache 2.0 licence, so anyone reading the specs can use what they find
there without a non-commercial restriction.

Why the split is in two files
-----------------------------

Both licences were previously stated in one summarised LICENSE. Licence
detectors match canonical text, so a summary carrying an extra clause matched
nothing: GitHub reported this repository as NOASSERTION ("Other") and every
registry recorded no licence at all. LICENSE now holds the unmodified Apache
2.0 text so it is detected, and this file carries the documentation terms.
Neither licence has been altered; only which file states them.
